{"id":"k-means-initialization","t":"K-means++ seeding","tags":["Clustering","Sampling"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"kmeans_pp","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"K-means is sensitive to where it starts. K-means++ picks starting centroids from the data so that they tend to be spread out: each new centre is sampled with probability proportional to its **squared distance to the nearest centre chosen so far**.\n\nWrite `kmeans_pp(X, k, seed)` that returns the **row indices** of the chosen centres, in the order they were chosen.\n\n## Procedure (follow exactly)\n1. `rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)`.\n2. First centre: `rng.integers(n)`.\n3. Keep $D_i^2$, the squared Euclidean distance from row $i$ to its nearest chosen centre.\n4. Each later centre: `rng.choice(n, p=D2 / D2.sum())`. Then update $D^2$ with the new centre.\n\n## Input / output\n- `X`: float `(n, d)` with at least `k` distinct rows; `1 <= k <= n`; `seed`: int.\n- Return an int array `(k,)`. No index repeats (a chosen row has $D^2 = 0$, so it can't be drawn again).\n- Updating $D^2$ must be $O(nd)$ per new centre: don't recompute distances to every earlier centre.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef kmeans_pp(X, k, seed):\n    \"\"\"Indices of k-means++ seeds in the order chosen. Returns an int array (k,).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["You only need one vector of length $n$: each point's squared distance to its **nearest** chosen centre.","After choosing centre $i$, the nearest-centre distance becomes `np.minimum(d2, dist2_to(X[i]))`. Nothing else needs recomputing.","Use the generator calls exactly as stated: `rng.integers(n)` once, then `rng.choice(n, p=d2 / d2.sum())` for each further centre. Any other call order draws different numbers."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef kmeans_pp(X, k, seed):\n    X = np.asarray(X, dtype=float)\n    n = len(X)\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    idx = [int(rng.integers(n))]\n    d2 = ((X - X[idx[0]]) ** 2).sum(axis=1)\n    for _ in range(1, k):\n        i = int(rng.choice(n, p=d2 / d2.sum()))            # far points are proportionally more likely\n        idx.append(i)\n        d2 = np.minimum(d2, ((X - X[i]) ** 2).sum(axis=1)) # nearest-centre distance only ever shrinks\n    return np.array(idx)\n","ex_md":"## Why squared distance\nSampling proportionally to $D^2$ (rather than $D$, or picking the farthest point) gives the $O(\\log k)$-competitive guarantee of Arthur and Vassilvitskii (2007): the expected inertia of the seeding alone is within $8(\\ln k + 2)$ of the optimum. The farthest-point rule has no such guarantee and is easily hijacked by outliers; $D^2$ sampling still favours far regions but outliers are just one point among many.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(nkd)$ total: each new centre costs one pass to compute distances to it and one `minimum`. `rng.choice` with `p` is $O(n)$.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Recomputing the distance to all previous centres each round, making it $O(nk^2d)$.\n- Using `D` instead of `D**2` as weights.\n- Sampling with a different generator call (for example `rng.random()` plus `searchsorted`). Mathematically equivalent, but it consumes the stream differently, so the indices differ.\n- Duplicate rows: test 4 has three identical rows; once one is chosen the others have $D^2 = 0$ too.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- K-means++ needs $k$ sequential passes. How does k-means|| (scalable k-means++) parallelise it? (Oversample about $\\ell = O(k)$ points per round for a few rounds, then recluster the candidates.)\n- Even with good seeding, why do libraries run k-means several times (`n_init`) and keep the best inertia?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[ 0. ,  0. ],\n       [ 0.1,  0. ],\n       [10. , 10. ],\n       [10. , 10.1]])\n2\n0","out":"array([3, 0])"},{"in":"array([[ 0.],\n       [ 1.],\n       [ 2.],\n       [50.]])\n3\n1","out":"array([1, 3, 0])"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../ml-maths-course/index.html#l10-25","t":"The Bernoulli Distribution","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"../machine-learning/index.html#l05-01","t":"k-means","c":"Machine Learning"}],"related":["k-means-clustering","kmeans-step"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 0.0], [0.1, 0.0], [10.0, 10.0], [10.0, 10.1]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 2, 0], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [3, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [1.0], [2.0], [50.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 3, 1], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [1, 3, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[3.0, 4.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1, 2], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [0.0], [0.0], [5.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 2, 3], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [3, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0], [9.0, 9.0], [9.0, 8.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 3, 4], \"e\": {\"__nd__\": [3, 1, 4], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-0.7334648730294828, -0.20981904965980783], [-0.02028437527218058, 0.1963987224206376], [-0.5173235410473758, 0.5936691550855768], [0.10874401285905771, -0.2663254980953532], [-0.7642144919371572, -0.33469677311418117], [0.35018279988962236, 0.2718682760653952], [0.442397574155967, -0.2584931716751395], [0.3784744192557596, -0.09825385157076151], [0.723110223306234, 0.04851340506017418], [-0.49740549995726274, -0.1562942938741585], [0.11465057582408678, 0.16104465869788015], [0.2527691723888954, -0.1877023658307325], [0.09979244033006318, 0.5865434646995757], [0.5318207590897734, 0.22090201801318862], [0.36164530267227774, -0.8015900507924009], [0.13488931393385992, 0.049403623648695424], [-0.1680782152470422, 0.11746153522975318], [-0.29662703280667896, -0.1028039398884386], [0.15982598818628807, -0.1535263510022037], [0.10658718329888232, 0.4261127951566932], [-0.700530918894254, -0.2798228652849511], [-0.9310849033931721, 0.4384007965639172], [0.4824185436161494, 0.3158806459109014], [0.3271045349749269, -0.22371556360712885], [0.7668465897574248, 0.4597133012190506], [-1.3560846820059071, 0.19342198